abstract: >-
  OpenIDEA Kiro is a verification gate for Specification-Driven Development with
  coding agents, delivered as a command-line tool for hooks and continuous
  integration and as a plugin for the OpenIDEA editor. It checks the specification:
  acceptance criteria written in the Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax (EARS)
  are grounded in a project vocabulary with topic scopes, turned into boolean
  guards over finite regions, and searched for contradictions with Reduced
  Ordered Binary Decision Diagrams (ROBDDs) via biodivine-lib-bdd. A contradiction
  is reported with a witness state, and the search refuses a problem beyond its
  resource bounds instead of guessing. It also checks the code that agents
  produce: limits on routines, types and files in Rust, Python, TypeScript and
  JavaScript, a ratchet that blocks only regression and new breaches, duplicate
  blocks, renamed copies of routines, definitions and state scattered over files,
  and data addressed by position. Every finding names the specification, design
  component and task that own the file and one edit that resolves it. The engine
  is a pure Rust crate that also runs as a WebAssembly (wasm32-wasip2) component.
